Given the function $f(x) = |x-a| + |x-2a|$,  
(Ⅰ) When $a=1$, find the solution set of the inequality $f(x) > 2$;  
(Ⅱ) If for any $x \in \mathbb{R}$, the inequality $f(x) \geq a^2 - 3a - 3$ always holds, find the range of values for $a$.

Solution:  
(Ⅰ) When $a=1$, $f(x) = |x-1| + |x-2|$.  
When $x \leq 1$, $f(x) = -x + 1 - x + 2 = 3 - 2x$, from the inequality $f(x) > 2$, we get $x < \frac{1}{2}$;  
When $1 < x < 2$, $f(x) = x - 1 - x + 2 = 1$, from the inequality $f(x) > 2$, we get $x \in \emptyset$;  
When $x \geq 2$, $f(x) = x - 1 + x - 2 = 2x - 3$, from the inequality $f(x) > 2$, we get $x > \frac{5}{2}$;  
Therefore, the solution set of the inequality $f(x) > 2$ is $(-\infty, \frac{1}{2}) \cup (\frac{5}{2}, +\infty)$;  

(Ⅱ) Since the inequality $f(x) \geq a^2 - 3a - 3$ always holds for $x \in \mathbb{R}$,  
thus, $f(x)_{\min} \geq a^2 - 3a - 3$,  
According to the triangle inequality of absolute values, $|x-a| + |x-2a| \geq |(x-a) - (x-2a)| = |a|$,  
i.e., $f(x)_{\min} = |a|$, hence, $|a| \geq a^2 - 3a - 3$, discussing in two cases:  
① When $a \geq 0$, $a \geq a^2 - 3a - 3$, i.e., $a^2 - 4a - 3 \leq 0$, thus $2 - \sqrt{7} \leq a \leq 2 + \sqrt{7}$, in this case $0 \leq a \leq 2 + \sqrt{7}$;  
② When $a < 0$, $-a \geq a^2 - 3a - 3$, i.e., $a^2 - 2a - 3 \leq 0$, thus $-1 \leq a \leq 3$, in this case $-1 \leq a < 0$.  
Combining the above discussions, the range of values for the real number $a$ is: $\boxed{[-1, 2 + \sqrt{7}]}$.

