Given the inequality $12x^{2}-ax > a^{2} (a \in \mathbb{R})$,

we have $12x^{2}-ax-a^{2} > 0$, which can be factored into $(4x+a)(3x-a) > 0$.

Let $(4x+a)(3x-a)=0$, we get $x=- \frac {a}{4}$ or $\frac {a}{3}$.

$(1)$ When $a > 0$, $- \frac {a}{4} < \frac {a}{3}$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{ \{x|x < - \frac {a}{4} \text{ or } x > \frac {a}{3} \} }$;

$(2)$ When $a=0$, $- \frac {a}{4} = \frac {a}{3}$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{ \{x|x \in \mathbb{R}, x \neq 0 \} }$;

$(3)$ When $a < 0$, $- \frac {a}{4} > \frac {a}{3}$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{ \{x|x > - \frac {a}{4} \text{ or } x < \frac {a}{3} \} }$.

In summary:

- When $a > 0$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{ \{x|x < - \frac {a}{4} \text{ or } x > \frac {a}{3} \} }$;
- When $a=0$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{ \{x|x \in \mathbb{R}, x \neq 0 \} }$;
- When $a < 0$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{ \{x|x > - \frac {a}{4} \text{ or } x < \frac {a}{3} \} }$.

