```javascript
const https = require('https');

function getPageData(url) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        https.get(url, response => {
            if (response.statusCode < 200 || response.statusCode > 299) {
               reject(new Error('Failed to load page, status code: ' + response.statusCode));
            }
            const body = [];
            response.on('data', chunk => body.push(chunk));
            response.on('end', () => resolve(body.join('')));
        });
    });
};
```

This function `getPageData` takes a `url` parameter and returns a `Promise` that resolves with the page data as a string. It uses the `https.get` method to make an HTTP GET request to the specified `url`. 

Inside the request callback, we check if the response status code is within the 2xx range. If it's not, we reject the promise with an error indicating the failure.

We then create an empty `body` array to store the received data chunks. The `response.on('data')` event is triggered multiple times as data chunks are received. In each `data` event, we push the chunk into the `body` array.

Finally, when the `response` emits the `end` event, we join all the data chunks in the `body` array into a single string using `body.join('')` and resolve the promise with the resulting page data.

